No user-facing change: the declaration-style and format incompatibility
contracts, the special-float rejections and the invalid-dict-key
rejections are declared as rejection manifests rather than written as
unit tests, so each is expanded across the languages its gates admit and
its message is kept in a golden file.  Only the cases that call an option
member's own callable, rather than going through ``literalize`` or a
constructor, remain hand-written.
